Priya Menon bought her first fitness watch to help her train for a half marathon. Within a week she had stopped thinking of it as a gadget and started thinking of it as a kind of personal assistant, one that congratulated her on early nights and gently scolded her for skipping runs.

{{41}}

The email that changed her mind was an ordinary one. A company she had never heard of wrote to say that, because of her "active lifestyle", she might be interested in a discount on sleep supplements. She had never mentioned sleep problems to anyone. She had only worn the watch to bed.

{{42}}

Priya, who works as a statistician, did what statisticians do: she went looking for the numbers. She requested a copy of everything the watch company held about her, a right she had under data protection law. The file that arrived three weeks later ran to more than two thousand pages.

{{43}}

Most of it was dull, as she had expected. Heart rate every few seconds, step counts, the route of every run. But buried in the detail were patterns she found harder to dismiss. The data showed when she was awake at three in the morning, and it showed which weeks had been stressful at work.

{{44}}

The company, when she contacted it, was polite and unapologetic. Her information, it explained, was shared with "trusted partners" in an anonymised form. Priya was unconvinced. Anonymised data, she knew, can often be traced back to a person if enough different details are combined, and a heart-rate pattern is about as individual as a fingerprint.

{{45}}

She has not thrown the watch away. She still runs, and she still likes knowing how well she has slept. What has changed is that she now turns off most of its features, reads the small print before she accepts an update, and no longer wears it at night.

